From Thirteen to Fifty (十三到五十): credits

History: OpenHistoricalMap, CC0 1.0 (public domain). https://www.openhistoricalmap.org
  The borders of the states, the territories and the United States with their start and end dates, and railways with
  their opening years. Fetched 2026-11-11 through OpenHistoricalMap's Overpass API by neo/geo/ohm.py; data/us.json is
  the part this film draws (made by tools/prep.py).
Base map: (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, Open Database License (ODbL) 1.0. https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright
  Land from OpenStreetMap's simplified land polygons (osmdata.openstreetmap.de); named rivers, the Great Lakes and Great
  Salt Lake, and peaks, through the Overpass API by neo/geo/osm.py. Fetched 2026-11-11. data/land.json, water.json,
  lakes-*.json and peaks-*.json are a derived database and stay under ODbL 1.0. The film is a produced work: it credits
  both sources on its last card.
  View: North America from 22 to 52 N, Albers equal-area conic (standard parallels 29.5 and 45.5 N, origin 96 W 37.5 N).
  The film is about how borders changed. Only the borders of the United States, its states and its territories are
  drawn, each for the years OpenHistoricalMap gives it; land outside them is bare paper with no line.

Picture: drawn entirely in code (Canvas 2D). No images, video or 3D models are used.
Sound: the score (banjo, guitar and bass by the library's core/audio/pluck.py string model; harmonica and foot drum
       synthesized in mix.py), every sound effect and the ambience are synthesized. No samples or recordings are used.

Fonts:
- IM Fell English, roman and italic. Igino Marini. SIL Open Font License 1.1. Ships with the library (core/fonts/).
- Noto Serif SC. Google. SIL Open Font License 1.1. Fetched by tools/fonts.sh into fonts/, not kept in git.
  https://github.com/google/fonts/tree/main/ofl/notoserifsc

Tools: the NEO-Cine library's core/ tools, which are Lemo-Opuscar's (MIT, (c) 2026 LemoLab): headless Chrome
frame capture, ffmpeg encode and loudness master.

Form reference (grammar only, none of their plates, ornaments or titles are used): nineteenth-century American
engraved and lithographed atlases, and wall maps on rollers.

An unofficial film about real history; the dates on screen are checked in FACTS.md. The wagon and the trains are
guides, not records: their routes follow the National Road, the Oregon Trail and the first transcontinental line
through well-known places, with approximate positions.
